shed":"2026-07-01","filename":"E07000105-ashford-2026-07-09-cabinet-34319-financial-monitoring-quarterly-report-hr.pdf"},{"id":32409,"title":"2026-27 Draft Budget Cabinet Report","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s32409/2026-27-draft-budget-cabinet-report.pdf","type":"budget","type_label":"Budget","weight":3,"committee":"Cabinet","meeting_id":4918,"meeting_date":"2025-12-11","published":"2025-12-03","filename":"E07000105-ashford-2025-12-11-cabinet-32409-2026-27-draft-budget-cabinet-report.pdf"},{"id":32936,"title":"Report of Overview and Scrutiny Budget Scrutiny Task Group","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s32936/report-of-overview-and-scrutiny-budget-scrutiny-task-group.pdf","type":"budget","type_label":"Budget","weight":3,"committee":"Cabinet","meeting_id":4920,"meeting_date":"2026-02-12","published":"2026-02-04","filename":"E07000105-ashford-2026-02-12-cabinet-32936-report-of-overview-and-scrutiny-budget-s.pdf"},{"id":32941,"title":"Revenue Budget 2026/27","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s32941/revenue-budget-2026-27.pdf","type":"budget","type_label":"Budget","weight":3,"committee":"Cabinet","meeting_id":4920,"meeting_date":"2026-02-12","published":"2026-02-04","filename":"E07000105-ashford-2026-02-12-cabinet-32941-revenue-budget-2026-27.pdf"},{"id":32780,"title":"BudgetTaskGroupReport supplement","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s32780/budgettaskgroupreport-supplement.pdf","type":"other","type_label":"Other finance papers","weight":1,"committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","meeting_id":5035,"meeting_date":"2026-01-27","published":"2026-01-19","filename":"E07000105-ashford-2026-01-27-overview-and-scrutiny-comm-32780-budgettaskgroupreport-supplement.pdf"}],"notes":{"gss":"E07000105","schema":2,"council":"Ashford","facts":[{"key":"loan_book","value":"18 PWLB loans totalling \u00a3171,050,357 at a weighted 3.5835% (17 HRA \u00a3159,425,357 + 1 GF \u00a311,625,000)","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Appendix A page 20 (totals) and page 21 (loan-by-loan). Our file (price_book.py against bc-loans) ties to the pound on both the HRA sub-total (\u00a3159,425,357) and the GF sub-total (\u00a311,625,000), 17 HRA rows and 1 GF row. The paper does not state a weighted rate to compare \u2014 3.5835% is our figure."},{"key":"short_dated_pwlb_matured","value":"\u00a330m HRA PWLB loan 792478 at 4.87% drawn 21 January 2025 for 16 months, matured 21 May 2026","as_at":"2026-05-21","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Appendix A page 21, row 17. A 16-month PWLB maturity loan for \u00a330m is an unusual shape for the PWLB. As at the Cabinet meeting date (9 July 2026) it was no longer outstanding \u2014 our live book shows 17 loans."},{"key":"temporary_borrowing","value":"\u00a3108,500,000 across 24 loans from 21 different LA-market lenders at rates from 3.75% to 7.00%","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Appendix A page 20 debt portfolio. 21 unique counterparties: 14 councils, 3 combined authorities, 3 fire and rescue services and 1 pension fund. Together with \u00a3171,050,357 long-term PWLB this makes a Grand Total Borrowing of \u00a3279,550,357 (paper Appendix A page 20 total). The short book is 38.8% of external borrowing."},{"key":"year_end_la_squeeze","value":"\u00a37.5m drawn in March 2026 at 6-7%: \u00a32m Nottingham FRS 06/03/2026 6.00% (32d); \u00a32m South Holland 09/03/2026 6.00% (39d); \u00a31.5m Boston 23/03/2026 7.00% (25d); \u00a32m Dartford 24/03/2026 7.00% (31d)","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Appendix A page 20 debt portfolio. Terms 25-39 days. Visible year-end squeeze in the LA money market."},{"key":"interest_payable","value":"\u00a35.051m outturn, \u00a3683k over current budget of \u00a34.368m","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Table 7 page 16 \u2014 original budget \u00a34.452m, current budget \u00a34.368m. Para 46 narrative gives \u00a3684k rounded pressure; Table 7 variance figure is \u00a3683k."},{"key":"interest_receivable","value":"\u00a34.415m outturn, \u00a3521k above current budget of \u00a33.894m","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Table 7 page 16. Para 48 attributes the surplus to higher-than-forecast Money Market Fund returns because \"interest rates did not fall as quickly as forecast, with the inverse being higher borrowing costs\"."},{"key":"mrp","value":"\u00a31.749m outturn against budget \u00a32.006m (\u00a3257k under)","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Table 7 page 16. MRP policy not discussed in the paper."},{"key":"investments","value":"\u00a333.669m at cost / \u00a332.221m fair value; long-term excluding subsidiaries \u00a330.000m cost / \u00a329.671m fair value (\u00a3329k below cost)","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Appendix A page 19 investment portfolio, with paras 49\u201350. \u00a31.203m in five ICD-portal MMFs at ~4%; \u00a311m CCLA LAPF; six strategic funds \u00a319m book. Portfolio \u00a3329k below original value ties: \u00a330,000,000 - \u00a329,670,509 = \u00a3329,491."},{"key":"cfr_and_external_borrowing","value":"not stated \u2014 this paper does not publish a CFR figure, an external borrowing summary line, an other-long-term-liabilities figure, or an internal borrowing figure","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Absence recorded. The balance-sheet identity (CFR \u2212 external borrowing \u2212 OLTL = internal borrowing) cannot be tested from this paper. The council's Treasury Management Annual Report would be the paper to hold; it is not appended here."},{"key":"gf_outturn","value":"GF deficit \u00a3183,000 (Q3 forecast \u00a3650,000 deficit); reserves +\u00a37.1m to closing \u00a340.290m","as_at":"2026-03-31","source":{"title":"Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316)","url":"http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf","doc_id":34316,"text":"papers/E07000105/34316.txt"},"note":"Front summary and Table 1 (page 4); reserves at Table 4 (page 10). Bank Rate over the year: 4.50% at start \u2192 3.75% at end (para 4)."}],"threads":[],"meetings":{},"summary":{"written":"2026-08-15","text":"<p>The GF outturn for 2025/26 closed at a \u00a3183,000 deficit against a Q3 forecast of \u00a3650,000, and general reserves rose \u00a37.1m to \u00a340.290m. The revenue commentary is the substance of the report; treasury sits at Table 7 (page 16) and Appendix A (pages 19\u201321).</p><p>Where this paper is unusually useful is Appendix A. It publishes the full PWLB book loan by loan with reference, advance date, maturity, amount and rate, and it publishes the entire \u00a3108.5m temporary-borrowing schedule with counterparty, rate and maturity. Our file matches the paper's PWLB totals to the pound \u2014 18 loans, \u00a3171,050,357, split HRA \u00a3159,425,357 and GF \u00a311,625,000. The one moving part is the \u00a330m HRA loan at 4.87% drawn 21 January 2025: a 16-month PWLB shape that matured on 21 May 2026, so by the meeting date it was off the book. Our live schedule shows 17 loans.</p><p>The temporary book is where the story is. At year-end \u00a3108.5m sat under twelve months across 21 different LA-market counterparties at rates from 3.75% up to 7.00% \u2014 38.8% of external borrowing under a year. The year-end squeeze is visible in the same table: \u00a37.5m drawn during March 2026 at 6-7% for terms of 25 to 39 days (\u00a32m Nottingham FRS 6.00%, \u00a32m South Holland 6.00%, \u00a31.5m Boston 7.00%, \u00a32m Dartford 7.00%). Interest payable finished \u00a3683k over budget (Table 7).</p><p>The paper does not publish CFR, other long-term liabilities or an internal borrowing figure, so the balance-sheet identity cannot be tested from it. No S151 or CFO is named \u2014 the only officer contacts are Emma Joyce (Senior Accountant, report author) and Lee Foreman (no title given).</p>"},"courses":{"scored":false,"read":["outturn"],"text_held":true},"contacts":[{"name":"Emma Joyce","role":"Senior Accountant","email":"Emma.Joyce@ashford.gov.uk","note":"Report author. Title verbatim from page 1. NOT the S151 \u2014 the paper does not name a Section 151 Officer or Chief Financial Officer."},{"name":"Lee Foreman","email":"Lee.Foreman@ashford.gov.uk","note":"Second contact on pages 2 and 18. No title published in the report. Do not assume a title."},{"name":"Cllr Ovenden","role":"Leader of the Council & Cabinet Member for Finance and Resource","note":"Portfolio Holder \u2014 a member, not an officer. Do not email."}],"status":{"state":"ready","written_up":true,"contact":true,"as_at":"2026-08-15","source_text_held":true,"note":"PWLB loan book ties to the pound (18 loans, \u00a3171,050,357). Balance-sheet identity NOT provable from this paper (no CFR, no OLTL, no internal-borrowing figure). Cabinet meeting has passed (9 July 2026); next known finance milestone is the audit sign-off in February 2027, with a Q1 2026/27 monitoring report likely in autumn 2026 (date not published). Draft addressed to Emma Joyce (Senior Accountant, report author) with Lee Foreman on CC \u2014 no S151 named. Titles taken verbatim from the paper."},"emails":{"officer":{"subject":"18 loans, \u00a3171,050,357 tied to your Appendix A \u2014 and \u00a330m matured in May","body":"Hi Emma,\n\nApologies for the cold email. PWLBtoday is fairly new so you may not have come across us yet.\n\nWe're built for local authority treasury teams and nothing else: live PWLB rates at both daily fixings, a monthly breakdown of every loan the sector draws, every council's PWLB book priced daily against the DMO's repayment curve, and a training academy for officers and members. There's more \u2014 the predictor, the SONIA path, peer metrics, a daily brief \u2014 in the brochure at [BROCHURE URL]. We don't sell treasury products, and the data side is free and stays free.\n\nTo keep that data and our training relevant we built tools that watch committee papers across every council in the country, so we can see how a council's own figures compare with where the market has gone since. Your outturn went to Cabinet on 9 July and we've only just got to it \u2014 so below are a few thoughts on the treasury pages, offered ahead of Q1 monitoring. You're not being singled out \u2014 every authority gets one of these \u2014 and there's no agenda in it. Appendix A is unusually helpful for an outturn: full loan-by-loan detail on both the PWLB and the temporary book, at the level most reports save for the Treasury Management Annual Report.\n\nOne cross-check first, so you can see our figures tie to yours. We hold all 18 of your PWLB loans \u2014 \u00a3171,050,357 at a weighted 3.58% \u2014 which is Appendix A's \u00a3159,425,357 HRA total and \u00a311,625,000 GF total exactly, and the 17 HRA rows and one GF row at 1.56% each match to the pound.\n\nThe thing that stands out is the maturity that fell on the seam. The \u00a330m at 4.87% you drew from the PWLB on 21 January 2025 matured on 21 May 2026, so by the time this report went to Cabinet it was already off the book. A 16-month PWLB maturity for \u00a330m is a shape most councils don't take at the PWLB at all. Since May the one-year certainty rate has come down a touch to 4.95% (14 August, notice 314/26 PM), so a like-for-like PWLB roll would land close to where it came off. The shape of the temporary book at year-end shows where the replacement is going: \u00a3108.5m under twelve months across 24 loans from 21 different LA-market counterparties, at rates from 3.75% up to 7.00%.\n\nAnd you can see the year-end squeeze in the same table. Four short loans drawn in March \u2014 \u00a32m from Nottingham FRS at 6.00%, \u00a32m from South Holland at 6.00%, \u00a31.5m from Boston at 7.00% and \u00a32m from Dartford at 7.00% \u2014 for terms of 25 to 39 days. That's \u00a37.5m at rates the LA market doesn't show outside March. Interest payable finished \u00a3683k over budget (Table 7), and part of that shape is what the temporary book carries.\n\nNothing in that needs a meeting or a subscription. The rates page at pwlbtoday.org/dashboard takes ten seconds \u2014 both fixings, whole curve. Your loan book, priced daily against the DMO repayment curve so premiums and discounts are a lookup rather than a request, is at pwlbtoday.org/loan-analysis. And the monthly sector breakdown is at pwlbtoday.org/pwlb-activity \u2014 July was \u00a3368m across 33 loans from 19 borrowers at an average 5.02%, which is a useful read on how thin the market is before you replace short LA money.\n\nOn break costs \u2014 for what it's worth, the 17 live loans price at an indicative net discount of about \u00a311.9m on our engine (8.5% of principal) against the DMO's premature repayment curve at notice 314/26 PM. All 17 sit below par. That is indicative and not a quote \u2014 only the DMO can quote, and we don't sell treasury products or model MRP or accounting treatment. Whether anything is worth doing turns on replacement cost we don't model.\n\nOn training, three fit where Ashford is: Interest rate risk, for the shape of the short book and the year-end LA market; our liability benchmark pathway, which is interactive and built on live data \u2014 worth a look given where CIPFA is heading with the Prudential and Treasury Codes; and Audit Committee Chair (Treasury) for members ahead of the accounts sign-off in February. All 115 courses are free to local authorities until the end of September at pwlbtoday.org/academy, and free accounts for the whole team.\n\nAnd if you do find it useful, mentioning it to others would help us more than anything. We're small, word of mouth is most of our marketing, and every sign-up helps us prove the need.\n\nSign up free \u2192 https://pwlbtoday.org/\nFollow on LinkedIn \u2192 https://www.linkedin.com/company/pwlbtoday/\n\nRussell\nhttps://www.linkedin.com/in/russelltew\n\nSource: Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund, Cabinet, 9 July 2026 \u2014 http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf.","to":"Emma Joyce, Senior Accountant <Emma.Joyce@ashford.gov.uk>","cc":"Lee Foreman <Lee.Foreman@ashford.gov.uk>","written":"2026-08-15","send_by":"2026-09-25","meeting":"Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (past); next known finance milestone is the audit sign-off with Audit Committee in February 2027 (para 55). Q1 2026/27 monitoring report expected in autumn 2026, date not published in the paper.","state":"ready","market":{"as_at":"2026-08-14","pwlb_notice":"314/26 (PM fixing)","basis":"MATURITY profile, CERTAINTY rates (published standard less 0.20)","pwlb_1y":4.95,"pwlb_2y":5.06,"pwlb_5y":5.34,"pwlb_10y":5.83,"pwlb_20y":6.38,"pwlb_25y":6.46,"pwlb_50y":6.27,"bank_rate":3.75,"ois_1y":4.11,"ois_2y":4.21,"note":"Bank Rate 3.75% at Ashford's year-end (para 4) and unchanged since. The \u00a330m HRA loan at 4.87% matured 21 May 2026; the one-year PWLB certainty rate on 14 August is 4.95%.","july_activity":"\u00a3368m across 33 loans from 19 borrowers, average 5.02%"},"voice":"Russell Tew, first-person. Deliberately not templated: no bold headers, no bulleted summary, contractions throughout. Follows outreach/EMAIL_TEMPLATE.md.","contacts_source":"Report author block (page 1) and contact lines on pages 2 and 18. Emma Joyce's title (Senior Accountant) is taken verbatim. Lee Foreman is on CC with no title because none is published in the paper. No S151 or CFO is named in the paper; do not guess.","placeholders":["[BROCHURE URL] \u2014 supply before sending"],"links":["pwlbtoday.org/loan-analysis","pwlbtoday.org/pwlb-activity","pwlbtoday.org/dashboard","pwlbtoday.org/academy"],"courses_named":["rate-risk","liability-benchmark (pathway)","audit-chair"],"sources":["Financial Outturn 2025/26 \u2013 General Fund \u2014 Cabinet, 9 July 2026 (s34316) \u2014 http://ashford.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s34316/final-outturn-general-fund.pdf"]}}}}